-A session **claims** a ticket by assigning it to the dev driving the map, **first**, before any work, so concurrent sessions skip it. That assignee _is_ the claim: an open, unassigned ticket is unclaimed.
+Assign the ticket to the dev driving the map before work so others can see it is claimed. In a single-session workflow, this is enough. Assignment is a coordination marker, not an atomic lock: concurrent sessions, especially under the same account, cannot prove exclusive ownership by reading back that assignee. For concurrent work, use an existing coordinator to allocate distinct tickets one at a time, or a tracker-supported atomic claim that confirms which session acquired the ticket. If neither is available, coordinate ownership before starting the affected ticket; distinct tickets can still be worked in parallel.

 Blocking uses the tracker's **native** dependency relationship: essential because it renders the frontier _visually_ in the tracker's own UI, so the human sees what's takeable without opening the map. Only a tracker that lacks native blocking falls back to a body convention. A ticket is **unblocked** when every ticket blocking it is closed; the **frontier** is the open, unblocked, unclaimed children, the edge of the known.

